entry .Scaffold   how a row type is put into columns: a table definition which holds no data
  detail
      A `Scaffold[row, text]` is an ordered list of `Column[row, text]`s, built with
      `Scaffold[row](columns*)`: the row type is given, and the text type — `Text` or
      `Teletype` — is inferred from the titles. It is the stage before data: `#tabulate` applies
      it to a `List[row]` to make a `Tabulation`, and `#layout` solves the column widths of the
      titles alone, for a table which admits its rows one at a time.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      val scaffold: Scaffold[Person, Text] =
        Scaffold[Person]
          ( Column(t"Name")(_.name),
            Column(t"Age")(_.age) )

      scaffold.tabulate(List(Person(t"Poins", 30), Person(t"Hal", 16))).grid(40).render.to[List]
      ```

      A `Tabulable` instance for a type is a function returning its scaffold, so a type whose
      columns are not its fields — a record read from a schema, or computed display columns —
      is tabulated by giving one. The derived instance for a case class builds a scaffold with
      one column per field.
  refer escritoire.Column
  refer escritoire.Tabulation
  refer escritoire.Layout
  entry .apply   `Scaffold[row](columns*)`: a scaffold of the given columns, in order
      The row type is the one explicit type argument; the text type is inferred from the
      columns, which all share it. A title written as `t"…"` makes a `Scaffold[row, Text]`; one
      written as `e"…"` makes a `Scaffold[row, Teletype]`. The method takes an erased `Void`
      parameter between its two type-parameter lists only so that the row type can be given
      without the text type.
  entry #columns0   the columns, as given to the constructor
      The repeated parameter the scaffold was built from; `#columns` is the same columns as
      an array, which is what the layout engine reads.
  entry #columns   the columns, as an array, in order
  entry #titles   the title cells, one row of them, each title cut into its lines
      A title may contain `\n`, which makes a title of several lines; the titles are held
      as a single-element list so that they can be measured and rendered like any row.
  entry #cells   a row phrased through the columns, ahead of any layout
      `scaffold.cells(row)` reads every column's content from the row, cuts each into lines
      and measures their widths, giving a `Cells[text]`. The cells are independent of any
      width, so they can be tested against a `Layout` with `Layout#accommodates`, admitted
      with `Layout#extend`, and rendered against it later. Needs a `Text is Measurable`.
  entry #layout   the layout of the titles alone, at a width
      `scaffold.layout(width)` solves the column widths from the titles' claims and nothing
      else, for a table which is built incrementally: each row is then phrased with `#cells`
      and admitted with `Layout#extend`, which re-solves the widths only when the row's cells
      exceed what the layout has already seen. Needs a `TableStyle`, a `Text is Measurable`
      and an `Attenuation` in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      val scaffold = Scaffold[Person](Column(t"Name")(_.name), Column(t"Age")(_.age))

      val titles = scaffold.layout(40)
      val row = scaffold.cells(Person(t"Cleopatra", 38))
      titles.accommodates(row)           // false: the name is wider than the title
      val layout = titles.extend(row)    // re-solved with the wider claim
      layout.lines(row, Nil)             // the row's lines at the settled widths
      ```
  entry #tabulate   the scaffold applied to data: a `Tabulation`
      `scaffold.tabulate(rows)` reads every column of every row into text at once, and
      records each row's cell decorations, so the result refers to the row type only as its
      `Row` type member. The row count is taken once, when the tabulation is built.

entry .Column   one column of a table: its title, how it reads a cell from a row, and how it is sized
  detail
      A `Column[row, text]` is a title, a function `#get` from the row to the cell's text, a
      horizontal and a vertical alignment, a `Columnar` sizing strategy, and an optional
      per-row `#decorate` applied to the whole rendered cell. The factory `Column.apply` is
      the usual way to make one; the case-class constructor takes every field explicitly.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      val name: Column[Person, Text] = Column(t"Name")(_.name)
      val age: Column[Person, Text] = Column(t"Age", sizing = columnar.Collapsible(0.5))(_.age)
      val note: Column[Person, Text] =
        Column(t"Note", textAlign = TextAlignment.Justify, sizing = columnar.Paragraph): person =>
          t"${person.name} is ${person.age}"
      ```

      The lambda's result need not be text: the title fixes the text type, and the value is
      shown through that type's `Show`, so `_.age` gives a column of `Int`s shown as text.
      The default alignment comes from `Column.Alignment[cell]` — numbers right, text left —
      unless `textAlign` or `verticalAlign` is given.
  refer escritoire.Columnar
  refer escritoire.columnar
  refer escritoire.Scaffold
  entry .apply   `Column(title, textAlign, verticalAlign, sizing, decorate)(get)`: makes a column
      The first parameter list is the title and four optional settings; the second is the
      function from the row to the cell value, which is shown through the title's `Textual`
      type's `Show`. `textAlign` and `verticalAlign` default to the `Column.Alignment` given
      for the cell type (`topLeft` where none is defined); `sizing` defaults to
      `columnar.Paragraph`; `decorate` defaults to no decoration.

      `decorate` is a function from the row to an optional transformation of the rendered
      cell — content and padding together, after alignment — so a `Teletype` table can paint a
      whole cell's background by row, and the padding is painted too:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      val highlighted: Column[Person, Teletype] =
        Column
          ( e"Name",
            decorate = person => if person.age > 18 then (line: Teletype) => e"$Bold($line)" else Unset )
          ( _.name )
      ```

      The row type is inferred from the lambda or given explicitly as
      `Column[Person, Text, Text](t"Name")(_.name)`, whose three type arguments are the row,
      the cell value and the text type.
  entry .Alignment   the default text and vertical alignment for a cell type
    detail
        `Column.Alignment[cell]` is the alignment a column of `cell` values takes when
        `Column.apply` is given neither `textAlign` nor `verticalAlign`: `Byte`, `Short`, `Int`
        and `Long` are right-aligned, `Text` left, all at the top. The givens are in the
        companion, so a column of `Int`s is right-aligned without an import; `topLeft` is the
        default for any other type. It is contravariant in the cell type, so an alignment for
        `Any` serves every type.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

        Column[Person, Int, Text](t"Age")(_.age).textAlign    // TextAlignment.Right
        Column[Person, Text, Text](t"Name")(_.name).textAlign // TextAlignment.Left
        ```
    entry .topLeft   left-aligned, at the top: the default for a type with no alignment of its own
    entry .byte   `Byte` cells align right
    entry .short   `Short` cells align right
    entry .int   `Int` cells align right
    entry .long   `Long` cells align right
    entry .text   `Text` cells align left
    entry #text   the horizontal alignment: `Left`, `Right`, `Center` or `Justify`
    entry #vertical   the vertical alignment: `Top`, `Middle` or `Bottom`
  entry #title   the column's heading, in the table's text type
  entry #get   the cell's text for a row
      The function from the row to the cell, already shown as text. In a column made with
      `Column.apply`, this is the lambda composed with the cell type's `Show`.
  entry #textAlign   how a cell's lines sit within the column's width
  entry #verticalAlign   where a short cell sits in a row that other cells have made taller
  entry #sizing   the `Columnar` strategy which decides the column's claim on the width
  entry #decorate   a per-row transformation of the rendered cell, or `Unset` for none
      Applied by the renderer after alignment and padding, to the whole cell including its
      gutter, so a background painted here covers the cell edge to edge while the rules stay
      undecorated. The renderer knows nothing of styling: the function is in the table's own
      text type.
  entry #contramap   the same column reading from another row type
      `column.contramap(lambda)` adapts a column defined for one row type to any type from
      which `lambda` can produce that row, keeping the title, alignment, sizing and
      decoration. This is how a column defined once serves every type which can supply its
      value:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      val nameColumn: Column[Text, Text] = Column[Text, Text, Text](t"Name")(identity)
      val personName: Column[Person, Text] = nameColumn.contramap[Person](_.name)
      ```
  entry #retitle   the same column with another title
      For a shared table definition where one caller wants a different heading. The derived
      `Tabulable` for a case class uses it to title each field's column.

entry .Columnar   a column-sizing strategy: how a column claims width, and how a cell is fitted to it
  detail
      A `Columnar` answers two questions. `#flex` describes the column's claim on the table's
      width — its minimum and natural widths, its appetite for spare space, and whether it may
      collapse entirely — from the aggregate `Metrics` of every line the column will display.
      `#fit` then arranges one cell's lines into the width the table settled on: wrapping,
      chopping, shortening with an ellipsis, or leaving them as they are.

      The claim is a function of the aggregate metrics alone, which are the max-fold of each
      line's own, so a layout can carry the aggregate, admit a new row by folding its cells
      in, and know — through `#accommodates` — when a row cannot move the column at all. The
      strategies provided are in the `columnar` package: `Paragraph`, `ParagraphOrBreak`,
      `Fixed`, `Shortened` and `Collapsible`. The concept is open: a strategy of one's own is
      an object or class extending `Columnar` which implements `flex(metrics, maxWidth)` and
      `fit`.

      The widths are solved by tessellate's `Flex.solve`: while the columns' minima cannot fit
      the width, the collapsible column of lowest rank is dropped; the survivors then share
      the width in proportion to their weight, within their bounds.
  refer escritoire.columnar
  refer escritoire.Column
  entry .metrics   the aggregate intrinsic widths of some lines
      `Columnar.metrics(lines)` is the max-fold of each line's `Flow.metrics`: a `Metrics`
      whose `min` is the widest unbreakable word and whose `natural` is the widest line.
      Every strategy's claim derives from it. Needs a `Text is Measurable`.
  entry #flex   the column's claim on the width, from its content's metrics
      The abstract form, `flex(metrics, maxWidth)`, returns a tessellate `Flex`: the column's
      minimum and natural widths, its weight when spare space is shared, an optional maximum,
      and whether it collapses, with a rank saying how readily. The overload taking an array
      of lines measures them with `Columnar.metrics` first.
  entry #accommodates   whether a cell is already within the column's aggregate claim
      `accommodates(aggregate, cell)` is true when admitting a cell with intrinsic widths
      `cell` leaves the claim — and so the solved widths — unchanged: by default, when its
      minimum and natural widths are both within the aggregate's. A strategy whose claim
      ignores its content overrides it to `true`, as `columnar.Fixed` does.
  entry #fit   one cell's lines arranged into the width the column settled on
      `fit(lines, width, textAlign)` gives the lines a cell displays at `width`: wrapped,
      chopped, shortened or unchanged, according to the strategy. Needs a `Text is
      Measurable` and a `Hyphenation`, which the wrapping strategies use at soft breaks.

entry .columnar   the sizing strategies, each a `Columnar` to name in a `Column`'s `sizing`
  detail
      The strategies a column may be given, as `sizing = columnar.Paragraph`. They are
      objects and case classes, not givens, so `columnar.Paragraph` is named at the column;
      the package exists to keep the five names together. Every `Column` defaults to
      `Paragraph`.

      Which column goes first when the table is too narrow is decided by the strategies
      together: a `Collapsible` column is dropped before anything else is squeezed, highest
      threshold first; a `Paragraph` column shrinks to its longest word; a `Fixed` column never
      moves. Below the width at which the minima fit, the `Attenuation` in scope decides what
      happens.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Four columns at a width which holds them all
        code
            import soundness.*
            import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric
            import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation

            case class Codebase(name: Text, id: Text, lines: Int, description: Text)

            val codebases = List
              ( Codebase(t"Gossamer", t"gossamer", 4200, t"Statically-checked text operations"),
                Codebase(t"Escritoire", t"escritoire", 1900, t"Tabular layout for the terminal") )

            val scaffold = Scaffold[Codebase]
              ( Column(t"Name")(_.name),
                Column(t"Identifier", sizing = columnar.Collapsible(0.9))(_.id),
                Column(t"Lines", sizing = columnar.Collapsible(0.3))(_.lines),
                Column(t"Description", sizing = columnar.Paragraph)(_.description) )

            scaffold.tabulate(codebases).grid(80).render.to[List]
      frame
        label Narrower: the identifier, the most collapsible column, is dropped first
        code
            scaffold.tabulate(codebases).grid(60).render.to[List]
      frame
        label Narrower still: the description wraps, since a paragraph shrinks before a collapsible column with a low threshold goes
        code
            scaffold.tabulate(codebases).grid(44).render.to[List]
      frame
        label At the narrowest, the line count goes too, and only the name and the wrapped description remain
        code
            scaffold.tabulate(codebases).grid(30).render.to[List]
      ##
      ```
  refer escritoire.Columnar
  refer escritoire.Column
  entry .Paragraph   wraps at word boundaries; may shrink to its longest word, never grows past its longest line
      The default. Its claim is `Flex.content`: a minimum of the widest unbreakable word, a
      natural width of the widest line, and a weight equal to the difference, so a column with
      more to absorb takes more of the spare width. A cell is fitted with `Flow.wrap`, which
      breaks at spaces and at the `Hyphenation` in scope's soft breaks, and never below the
      minimum: a word longer than the width overflows rather than being cut.
  entry .ParagraphOrBreak   wraps at word boundaries where it can, and chops mid-word rather than overflow
      Elastic between a single cell and its natural width, so it has no longest-word floor:
      under enough pressure a cell narrower than its widest word is fitted with `Flow.chop`,
      cutting lines at the width, where `Paragraph` would overflow. For columns of
      identifiers, URLs or hashes which wrap badly but must not spill. It is not mirrored in
      the umbrella's `columnar` package, so it is named in full as
      `escritoire.columnar.ParagraphOrBreak`.
  entry .Fixed   a column exactly `fixedWidth` wide, whatever its content, shortened with an ellipsis
    detail
        The column's claim is rigid at `fixedWidth` and it accommodates every cell, since its
        width owes nothing to its content; a cell longer than the width is shortened with
        `Flow.shorten` and ends in `ellipsis` (`…` unless another is given).

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
        import textMetrics.uniformMetric
        import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation

        val fixed = Scaffold[Text](Column[Text, Text, Text](t"Fixed", sizing = columnar.Fixed(6))(identity))
        fixed.tabulate(List(t"abcdefghij")).grid(40).render.to[List]   // the cell shows as `abcde…`
        ```
    entry #fixedWidth   the width, in cells
    entry #ellipsis   the text which ends a shortened cell: `…` by default
  entry .Shortened   elastic between one cell and its natural width; whatever exceeds the settled width is cut with an ellipsis
    detail
        Like `Fixed`, a cell which does not fit is shortened with an ellipsis, but the width is
        negotiated rather than fixed: the column claims its natural width, may shrink to a
        single cell, and takes spare width in proportion to the difference. `fixedWidth` names
        the field but is not a bound on the claim.
    entry #fixedWidth   the field the strategy was constructed with
    entry #ellipsis   the text which ends a shortened cell: `…` by default
  entry .Collapsible   rigid at its natural width, but dropped from the table entirely when space runs out
    detail
        The column claims exactly its natural width and never wraps; when the columns' minima
        cannot fit the table's width, collapsible columns are dropped one by one, those with
        the highest `threshold` first, until the rest fit. A dropped column costs no padding
        and no rule. The thresholds are meaningful only relative to one another, so a table's
        collapsible columns are ranked by them:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
        import textMetrics.uniformMetric
        import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

        val collapsing = Scaffold[Person]
          ( Column(t"Name")(_.name),
            Column(t"Note", sizing = columnar.Collapsible(0.5))(_ => t"annotation") )

        val people = List(Person(t"Poins", 30), Person(t"Hal", 16))
        collapsing.tabulate(people).grid(30).render.to[List]   // both columns
        collapsing.tabulate(people).grid(14).render.to[List]   // the name alone
        ```
    entry #threshold   between `0` and `1`: how readily the column collapses, higher first

entry .Tabulation   data arranged by a scaffold: the cells' text, ready to be laid out at any width
  detail
      A `Tabulation[text]` is what `Scaffold#tabulate` (or the `tabulation` extension) makes
      from a list of rows: every cell already read into text and cut into lines, the titles
      likewise, and each row's decorations, with the row type surviving only as the `#Row`
      type member. It is independent of any width: `#grid` fits it to one, and `#layout`
      solves the widths without rendering.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      val people = List(Person(t"Poins", 30), Person(t"Hal", 16))

      val tabulation: Tabulation[Text] = people.tabulation
      tabulation.grid(40).render.to[List]
      tabulation.grid(12).render.to[List]
      ```

      A tabulation is `Printable` through `.printable`, so `Out.println(tabulation)` lays it
      out at the terminal's width with the style, metric, attenuation and hyphenation in
      scope.
  refer escritoire.Scaffold
  refer escritoire.Grid
  refer escritoire.Layout
  entry .printable   prints a tabulation at the terminal's width
      The `Printable` given, which lays the table out at `termcap.width` and prints each
      line through the text type's own `Printable`. It needs a `TableStyle`, a `Text is
      Measurable`, an `Attenuation` and a `Hyphenation` in scope, and the text type's
      `Printable`.
  entry #Row   the row type the tabulation was made from
  entry #columns   the scaffold's columns
  entry #titles   the title row: each title's lines
  entry #rows   every data row: each cell's lines
  entry #dataLength   the number of rows, counted once when the tabulation was built
  entry #decorations   each row's per-column decorations, aligned with `#rows`; empty for none
      Evaluated when the tabulation is built, the last point at which the row values exist.
  entry #layout   the solved column widths at a width, from the titles' and every row's claims
      `tabulation.layout(width)` folds every row's cells into the columns' aggregate claims
      and solves the widths once, giving a `Layout`. Its `widths` are the widths `#grid`
      renders with. Needs a `TableStyle`, a `Text is Measurable` and an `Attenuation`.
  entry #grid   the tabulation fitted to a width: a `Grid` whose `render` is the lines
      `tabulation.grid(width)` solves the layout as `#layout` does, fits every cell to its
      column's width, and builds a `Grid` of two sections — the titles and the rows — sharing
      the same widths. If the columns' minima exceed `width` even after every collapsible
      column has gone, the `Attenuation` in scope is told: `columnAttenuation.failAttenuation`
      raises `Table.Error` and `ignoreAttenuation` lets the table overflow. Needs a
      `TableStyle`, a `Text is Measurable`, an `Attenuation` and a `Hyphenation`.

entry .Grid   a table fitted to a width: sections of rows at settled column widths, in a style
  detail
      The last stage before text. A `Grid[text]` is a list of `TableSection`s — each a run of
      `TableRow`s with the column widths they share — and the `TableStyle` to draw them in.
      `Tabulation#grid` makes one with two sections, the titles and the data; `#render` draws
      it as a `Chain` of lines, with a rule between sections and the style's top and bottom
      lines around them.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      val grid: Grid[Text] = List(Person(t"Poins", 30)).tabulation.grid(40)
      grid.render.to[List]
      grid.sections.head.let(_.widths.to[List])   // the settled widths
      ```

      A grid is `Printable` through `.printable`, so it can be printed directly.
  refer escritoire.Tabulation
  refer escritoire.TableStyle
  refer escritoire.Layout
  entry .printable   prints a grid, one rendered line per terminal line
      The `Printable` given: `render` joined with newlines, each line printed through the
      text type's own `Printable`. Needs a `Text is Measurable` and the text type's
      `Printable`.
  entry .rowLines   one row's visual lines against its columns' widths
      `Grid.rowLines(style, widths, row)` is `row.height` lines: each cell padded to its
      column's width and aligned vertically, wrapped in the style's padding, decorated if the
      cell has a decoration, and joined with the style's edge glyphs. The gutter padding
      belongs to the cell, so a decoration covers the cell edge to edge while the rules stay
      bare. Used by `Grid#render` and `Layout#lines`.
  entry .rule   a horizontal rule across columns of the given widths
      `Grid.rule(style, widths, above, below)` draws a rule the width of the columns and their
      chrome, with a joint glyph wherever a column edge meets it; `above` and `below` say on
      which sides columns adjoin, so a top rule has only descenders, a bottom rule only
      ascenders, and the rule beneath the titles both. The line weight is the style's
      `topLine`, `bottomLine` or `titleLine` accordingly.
  entry #sections   the sections, in order, each with its rows and their shared widths
  entry #style   the style the grid is drawn in
  entry #render   the grid as lines of text
      A `Chain[text]`: the style's top line (if it has one), each section's rows with a rule
      between sections, and the bottom line (if any). Needs a `Text is Measurable`.

entry .Layout   a table's settled column widths, and the claims they were solved from
  detail
      A `Layout[row, text]` is the value between measuring a table and rendering it: which
      columns survived (`#survivors`, with their widths), the aggregate claims they were
      solved from, and the width and style they were solved at. It is immutable and
      incremental: a row phrased as `Cells` is tested with `#accommodates` — within every
      column's aggregate, it cannot move a column — and admitted with `#extend`, which gives
      the same layout when nothing moves and a re-solved one otherwise. Rows are then
      rendered one at a time with `#lines`, and a row's height is known from `#height`
      without rendering it when none of its cells wraps.

      This is the engine behind `Tabulation#grid`, exposed for a table which is built or
      displayed row by row — a log which grows, a screen which repaints — where re-laying the
      whole table for each row would be wasteful. `Scaffold#layout` makes the first layout
      from the titles alone. `Layout` is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella, so the type
      is named as `escritoire.Layout` where it must be written.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      val scaffold = Scaffold[Person](Column(t"Name")(_.name), Column(t"Age")(_.age))
      val people = List(Person(t"Poins", 30), Person(t"Hal", 16))

      val cells = people.map(scaffold.cells(_))
      val layout = cells.fold(scaffold.layout(40)) { (layout, cells) => layout.extend(cells) }

      layout.widths.to[List]
      layout.titleLines ++ cells.bind(layout.lines(_, Nil))
      ```
  refer escritoire.Scaffold
  refer escritoire.Cells
  refer escritoire.Tabulation
  entry .nothing   empty aggregates, one per column, for a layout which has admitted nothing yet
  entry .aggregate   the aggregates widened by a row's cells, column by column
      `Layout.aggregate(aggregates, cells)` is the elementwise `Metrics.max` of the two,
      so the result claims at least what either did.
  entry .solve   solves the column widths at a width from the aggregate claims
      `Layout.solve(columns, titles, aggregates, width, style)` asks each column's `Columnar`
      for its `Flex` from its aggregate, drops any column which can occupy no width at all (a
      `Paragraph` column whose every cell is empty, say), deducts the style's chrome, and lets
      tessellate's `Flex.solve` collapse and distribute the rest. If the surviving columns'
      minima still exceed the width, the `Attenuation` in scope is called with the minimum
      the table needed and the width it had. Needs a `Text is Measurable` and an
      `Attenuation`.
  entry #columns   the columns the layout was solved for
  entry #titles   the title rows' cells
  entry #aggregates   the per-column aggregate claims the widths were solved from
  entry #width   the width the layout was solved at
  entry #survivors   the columns which survived, as `(index, width)` pairs in order
      A column dropped by collapse is absent; `#widths` is the second element of each pair.
  entry #style   the style, whose padding and rules the chrome was costed from
  entry #widths   the surviving columns' widths, in order
  entry #cells   a row phrased through the layout's columns
      The same as `Scaffold#cells`: each column's content for the row, cut into lines and
      measured.
  entry #accommodates   whether a row's cells are within every column's aggregate claim
      True when admitting the row cannot change any column's claim, by each column's
      `Columnar#accommodates`; `#extend` then returns this very layout.
  entry #extend   this layout, or one re-solved with the row's cells folded in
      `layout.extend(cells)` is `layout` itself if `#accommodates` holds, and otherwise
      `Layout.solve` over the aggregates widened by the cells, at the same width. The
      re-solve may leave every width as it was — a wider claim on a column already pinned by
      the width changes nothing — which `#stable` detects. Needs a `Text is Measurable` and an
      `Attenuation`.
  entry #resize   the same claims solved at another width
  entry #stable   whether two layouts show the same columns at the same widths
      Compares the `#survivors`: a repaint can skip the rows already drawn when a layout
      extended by a new row is stable against the one they were drawn with.
  entry #tableRow   a row's cells fitted to the surviving columns' widths, as a `TableRow`
      `layout.tableRow(cells, decorations)` fits each surviving column's lines with its
      `Columnar#fit`, pairs each with its decoration (read from `decorations` by column
      position, `Unset` beyond its end), and measures the row's height. Needs a `Text is
      Measurable` and a `Hyphenation`.
  entry #lines   a row rendered against the layout: its lines of text
      `layout.lines(cells, decorations)` is `Grid.rowLines` over `#tableRow`: the row's
      visual lines, with the style's edges, as a `List`. Pass `Nil` for no decorations.
  entry #height   a row's height in lines, without rendering it where that is possible
      A cell whose natural width fits its column is not wrapped, so it stands as many lines
      as it has; only a cell which must wrap is fitted to find out. The row's height is the
      tallest cell's.
  entry #titleLines   the title rows rendered against the layout
  entry #topRule   the rule above the titles, or `Unset` if the style has no top line
  entry #titleRule   the rule between the titles and the rows
  entry #bottomRule   the rule beneath the last row, or `Unset` if the style has no bottom line

entry .Cells   one row's cells as the lines each will display, with each cell's intrinsic widths
  detail
      `Cells[text]` is a row phrased once from its row value — each column's content cut into
      lines, and each cell's `Metrics` (its longest word and its longest line) — and
      independent of any width, so a layout can test it with `Layout#accommodates`, admit it
      with `Layout#extend`, and render it at whatever widths it settles on. `Scaffold#cells`
      and `Layout#cells` make one from a row; `Cells.of` from lines already cut.

      `Cells` is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella: it is the layout engine's own
      vocabulary, reached as `escritoire.Cells` where a program drives a `Layout` directly.
  refer escritoire.Layout
  entry .of   cells from lines already cut, or from a row through its columns
      `Cells.of(lines)` measures each cell's lines; `Cells.of(columns, row)` reads each
      column's content from the row first. Both need a `Text is Measurable`.
  entry #lines   each cell's lines, by column
  entry #metrics   each cell's intrinsic widths, by column

entry .Columnation   a flat list arranged in columns, as `ls` lists a directory
  detail
      Not every arrangement of text in columns is a table. A list of short items — file
      names, the entries of a legend — reads best as `ls` lays out a directory: as many
      columns as the width admits, each item in one cell, no rules and no titles.
      `Columnation.layout` does this for a list of textual values; the `columnate` extension
      is the same operation as a method on the list.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric

      Columnation.layout(List(t"apple", t"fig", t"kiwi", t"lime", t"pear", t"plum"), 24)
      // List(t"apple  fig    kiwi  lime", t"pear   plum")
      ```
  refer escritoire.columnate
  entry .layout   the items in as many columns as fit a width, one line per row
      `Columnation.layout(items, width, gap, uniform, downward, align)` chooses the greatest
      column count at which every column's content fits alongside the others, with `gap`
      cells (two by default) between neighbours. Equal-width columns — each as wide as the
      widest item — are preferred, but a layout with one more column is admitted when the
      items which fall in some column happen to be narrower; spare width is then shared back
      out towards equality, no column growing beyond the widest item. `uniform = true`
      considers only equal-width layouts; `downward = true` reads the items down each column
      rather than across each row; `align` pads each cell within its column, and a
      left-aligned row carries no trailing padding after its last item. An item wider than
      the width still occupies a single column, and no items make no lines. Needs a `Text is
      Measurable`.

entry .columnate   `items.columnate(width)`: a list of text in as many columns as fit, one line per row
    An extension on `List[text]` for any `Textual` text with `Char` elements, which calls
    `Columnation.layout` with the same parameters: `gap` (default 2), `uniform`, `downward`
    and `align` (default `TextAlignment.Left`). Needs a `Text is Measurable`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import textMetrics.uniformMetric

    List(t"a", t"bb", t"c", t"dd", t"e").columnate(11)                       // List(t"a   bb  c", t"dd  e")
    List(t"aaaa", t"b", t"c", t"d").columnate(12, uniform = true)           // List(t"aaaa  b", t"c     d")
    List(t"a", t"b", t"c", t"d", t"e").columnate(7, downward = true)        // List(t"a  c  e", t"b  d")
    List(t"a", t"bb").columnate(10, align = TextAlignment.Right)            // List(t" a  bb")
    ```

entry .tabulation   `rows.tabulation`: a list tabulated by its element type's `Tabulable`, or a value by its `Tabular`
    Two extensions. On a `List[row]`, `rows.tabulation` needs a `row is Tabulable[text]` —
    derived for any case class, and given for `Int`, `Double` and `Text` — and gives the
    `Tabulation[text]` of the scaffold applied to the rows. On any value, `value.tabulation`
    needs a `value is Tabular[text]`, which supplies both the scaffold and the rows from the
    value itself. The text type is inferred from the instance, or given as
    `rows.tabulation[Text]`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
    import textMetrics.uniformMetric
    import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

    List(Person(t"Poins", 30), Person(t"Hal", 16)).tabulation.grid(40).render.to[List]
    List(1, 22, 333).tabulation.grid(20).render.to[List]   // one untitled, right-aligned column
    ```

entry .Tabulable   a typeclass: how a type is put into columns, so a list of it tabulates
  detail
      `row is Tabulable[text]` is a function `#table()` returning the `Scaffold[row, text]`
      for the type; `#tabulate` applies it to a list. Its companion derives an instance for
      any case class — a column per field, each titled by its field name uncamelled and
      capitalised (`linesOfCode` becomes `Lines Of Code`), unless a `TableRelabelling` for the
      type in scope says otherwise — and provides instances for `Int`, `Double` and `Text`,
      each a single untitled column. A field's column is the field type's own `Tabulable`
      scaffold, adapted to the record with `Column#contramap`, so a field of a case-class type
      contributes that type's columns in turn.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      summon[Person is Tabulable[Text]].tabulate(List(Person(t"Poins", 30))).grid(40).render.to[List]
      ```

      An instance of one's own is the seam for a type whose columns are not its fields:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      given Person is Tabulable[Text] = () =>
        Scaffold[Person](Column(t"Who")(_.name), Column(t"Adult?")(person => if person.age >= 18 then t"yes" else t"no"))
      ```

      Only `Text`-celled instances are derived: a `Teletype` table is written as a `Scaffold`
      by hand.
  refer escritoire.Scaffold
  refer escritoire.TableRelabelling
  refer escritoire.tabulation
  entry .JoinTabulable   the derived instance for a product type, over its fields' columns
    detail
        The class the derivation builds: a `Tabulable[Text]` whose scaffold is the given
        columns. Not normally named by a user; `conjunction` makes one.
    entry #table   the scaffold of the joined columns
  entry .conjunction   derives the instance for a case class, one column per field
      Each field's `Tabulable` gives its columns, which are adapted to the record and
      retitled from the field's label — through a `TableRelabelling[derivation]` if one is
      in scope, and otherwise uncamelled and capitalised. This is what the `derived` given
      inherited from wisteria's `ProductDerivation` calls, so a case class needs no
      annotation to be tabulated.
  entry .int   `Int` as one untitled, right-aligned, collapsible column
      The column collapses at threshold `0.3`, so a lone number is the first thing to go when
      a record of several fields is squeezed.
  entry .double   `Double` as one untitled, right-aligned, collapsible column
      Needs a `Decimalizer` in scope, which decides how many decimal places are shown.
  entry .text   `Text` as one untitled, left-aligned paragraph column
  entry #table   the scaffold for the type
  entry #tabulate   the scaffold applied to a list of the type: a `Tabulation`
      The scaffold is built once, lazily, and reused for every call.

entry .Tabular   a typeclass: a value which is itself a table, supplying both its columns and its rows
  detail
      Where `Tabulable` tabulates a *list* of a type, `value is Tabular[text]` tabulates a
      single value which contains its rows: `#table` gives the scaffold for its `#Element`
      type and `#rows` extracts the elements, and `#tabulate` applies one to the other. A
      report object, a query result or a matrix is tabulated by giving one, and then
      `value.tabulation` through the `tabulation` extension.
  refer escritoire.Tabulable
  refer escritoire.tabulation
  entry #Element   the row type the value's rows have
  entry #table   the scaffold for the value's rows
  entry #rows   the value's rows, as a list of `Element`
  entry #tabulate   the value as a `Tabulation`: `table(value).tabulate(rows(value))`

entry .TableRelabelling   overrides the column titles a derived table takes from its field names
  detail
      A `TableRelabelling[target]` is a function `#relabelling()` from field names to the
      titles wanted instead, consulted by the derived `Tabulable` for `target`: a field named
      in the map takes the mapped title, and any other its uncamelled, capitalised name. A
      given in scope at the point of derivation is enough; the trait is covariant in its
      target, so a relabelling for a supertype applies to its subtypes.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      given TableRelabelling[Person] = () => Map(t"name" -> t"Full Name")

      List(Person(t"Poins", 30)).tabulation.grid(40).render.to[List].head   // the title is `Full Name`
      ```
  refer escritoire.Tabulable
  entry #relabelling   the map from field names to titles
      Called once and cached; a field absent from the map keeps its derived title.
  entry #apply   the title for a field name, or `Unset` if the map does not name it

entry .Table   the namespace of `Table.Error`, raised when a table cannot fit its width
  detail
      `Table` holds nothing but the error. There is no `Table` type: a table definition is a
      `Scaffold`, data arranged by one a `Tabulation`, and the fitted result a `Grid`.
  refer escritoire.Scaffold
  refer escritoire.columnAttenuation
  entry .Error   the table needed more width than it was given
    detail
        _the table required a minimum width of `minimumWidth`, but only `availableWidth` was
        available_. Raised through `columnAttenuation.failAttenuation` when, after every
        collapsible column has been dropped, the surviving columns' minimum widths and the
        style's chrome still exceed the width a grid or layout was asked for. The limit is
        concrete: a `Paragraph` column can never be narrower than its longest word, so a table
        whose description column holds `Statically-checked` cannot fit below that word's width
        plus the rest. Under `ignoreAttenuation` the error is never raised and the table
        overflows instead.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
        import textMetrics.uniformMetric
        import columnAttenuation.failAttenuation

        val wide = Scaffold[Text](Column[Text, Text, Text](t"Name", sizing = columnar.Fixed(20))(identity))

        recover:
          case Table.Error(minimum, available) => List(t"needs $minimum, had $available")
        . protect:
            wide.tabulate(List(t"Poins")).grid(5).render.to[List]
        ```
    entry #minimumWidth   the narrowest width at which the table's columns could all fit
    entry #availableWidth   the width the table was asked to fit

entry .Attenuation   what happens when a table's columns cannot fit the width it was given
  detail
      An `Attenuation` is called by `Layout.solve` with the minimum width the table needed and
      the width it had, when the first exceeds the second even after every collapsible column
      has been dropped. It is a policy, not a behaviour the library fixes: a report written
      to a file, where nobody will see that the columns were mangled, wants a failure; an
      interactive display, where the user can widen the window, wants the rendering. The two
      policies are the givens of the `columnAttenuation` package, imported by name; every
      `grid`, `layout`, `extend` and `resize` needs one in scope.
  refer escritoire.columnAttenuation
  refer escritoire.Table
  entry #apply   called with the minimum width the table needed and the width available

entry .columnAttenuation   the two policies for a table which does not fit, each a given to import by name
  detail
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation
      ```

      `ignoreAttenuation` renders the table anyway, as well as the width allows, and cannot
      fail, so choosing it needs no error handling; `failAttenuation` raises `Table.Error`
      rather than produce something misleading, and so needs a `Tactic[Table.Error]` — a
      `recover`, a `safely`, or `strategies.throwUnsafely` — in scope. Neither is in scope
      without an import, since `import soundness.*` imports no givens.
  refer escritoire.Attenuation
  refer escritoire.Table
  entry .failAttenuation   raises `Table.Error` when the table cannot fit
      A context function from a `Tactic[Table.Error]` to an `Attenuation` which captures it,
      so importing the given also demands the tactic where the table is laid out. The error
      carries the minimum width needed and the width available.
  entry .ignoreAttenuation   renders the table anyway, overflowing the width
      Does nothing when told the table does not fit: the surviving columns keep their minimum
      widths and the lines run past the width asked for.

entry .TableStyle   the rules, corners and padding a table is drawn with
  detail
      A `TableStyle` says how much padding each cell has on either side, which weight of line
      — `Blank`, `Thin`, `Thick` or `Double`, or `Unset` for no line at all — draws the top
      edge, the bottom edge, the rule beneath the titles, the outer sides and the inner column
      edges, and which `LineCharset` (`Default`, `Rounded` or `Ascii`) supplies the glyphs.
      The six styles in the `tableStyles` package cover the usual choices; a style of one's
      own is a `TableStyle` value given locally.

      A style is a contextual value: `Tabulation#grid`, `Scaffold#layout` and the `Printable`
      givens take one, and `import soundness.*` brings none in, so one of the `tableStyles`
      givens is imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given ascii: TableStyle =
        TableStyle(1, BoxLine.Thin, BoxLine.Thin, BoxLine.Thin, BoxLine.Thin, BoxLine.Thin, LineCharset.Ascii)
      ```

      `Blank` and `Unset` differ: a `Blank` top line is drawn as a blank line, keeping the
      table's vertical spacing, where `Unset` draws nothing. Styled `Teletype` content lays
      out by its visible width in every style.
  refer escritoire.tableStyles
  refer escritoire.Grid
  entry #padding   the cells to the left and right of each cell's content
  entry #topLine   the weight of the top edge, or `Unset` for no top edge
  entry #bottomLine   the weight of the bottom edge, or `Unset` for no bottom edge
  entry #titleLine   the weight of the rule between the titles and the rows, or `Unset` for none
  entry #sideLines   the weight of the outer left and right edges; `Blank` for none
  entry #innerLines   the weight of the edges between columns; `Blank` for none
  entry #charset   which glyph set draws the lines: `Default`, `Rounded` or `Ascii`
  entry #columnCost   the chrome one column adds: its padding on both sides and one edge
  entry #cost   the chrome of a given number of columns: their costs and the closing edge

entry .tableStyles   six ready-made table styles, each a given to import by name
  detail
      The box drawing is chosen by the `TableStyle` given in scope, and the choice is never
      implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of these in, and one is imported by name. The
      same table under the six styles:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import columnAttenuation.ignoreAttenuation

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      val people = List(Person(t"Poins", 30), Person(t"Hal", 16))
      ```

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Thick
        code
            locally:
              import tableStyles.thickTableStyle
              people.tabulation.grid(40).render.to[List]
              // ┏━━━━━━━┯━━━━━┓
              // ┃ Name  │ Age ┃
              // ┠───────┼─────┨
              // ┃ Poins │  30 ┃
              // ┃ Hal   │  16 ┃
              // ┗━━━━━━━┷━━━━━┛
      alternative Thin rounded
        code
            locally:
              import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
              people.tabulation.grid(40).render.to[List]
              // ╭───────┬─────╮
              // │ Name  │ Age │
              // ├───────┼─────┤
              // │ Poins │  30 │
              // │ Hal   │  16 │
              // ╰───────┴─────╯
      alternative Horizontal
        code
            locally:
              import tableStyles.horizontalTableStyle
              people.tabulation.grid(40).render.to[List]
              // ╶─────────────╴
              //   Name    Age
              // ╶─────────────╴
              //   Poins    30
              //   Hal      16
              // ╶─────────────╴
      alternative Vertical
        code
            locally:
              import tableStyles.verticalTableStyle
              people.tabulation.grid(40).render.to[List]
      alternative Mid only
        code
            locally:
              import tableStyles.midOnlyTableStyle
              people.tabulation.grid(40).render.to[List]
      alternative Minimal
        code
            locally:
              import tableStyles.minimalTableStyle
              people.tabulation.grid(40).render.to[List]
              //   Name    Age
              // ╶─────────────╴
              //   Poins    30
              //   Hal      16
      ##
      ```

      A style of one's own is a `TableStyle` value bound as a local given instead.
  refer escritoire.TableStyle
  entry .thickTableStyle   heavy top, bottom and sides; thin title rule and column edges; square corners
  entry .thinRoundedTableStyle   thin rules throughout, with rounded corners
  entry .horizontalTableStyle   thin top, bottom and title rules; no vertical lines
      The cells keep their padding, so columns are separated by two spaces where the edge
      would have been.
  entry .midOnlyTableStyle   a thin rule beneath the titles; blank lines above and below the table
      The top and bottom lines are `Blank`, not absent, so the table keeps the vertical
      spacing a bordered one would have without drawing the border.
  entry .verticalTableStyle   thin side and column edges; no horizontal rules
      The horizontal rules are `Blank`, so each is a line of edge stubs: `╷` above and `╵`
      below.
  entry .minimalTableStyle   a thin rule beneath the titles and nothing else
      The top and bottom lines are `Unset`, so the table is its title line, one rule, and
      its rows.

entry .TextAlignment   how a cell's lines sit within a column's width: tessellate's `Alignment`
    `TextAlignment` is an alias for `tessellate.Alignment`, with `TextAlignment` the value
    `tessellate.Alignment` so that the cases are named as `TextAlignment.Left`. `Left` pads
    on the right; `Right` pads on the left; `Center` splits the padding, putting the odd cell
    on the right; `Justify` spreads the spaces between words so that every line but the last
    reaches the full width — the newspaper setting, which suits a prose column and nothing
    else. Each has a `pad(content, width, last)` which does the padding, `last` saying whether
    the line is a cell's last, which `Justify` leaves ragged.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import textMetrics.uniformMetric

    TextAlignment.Left.pad(t"hi", 6, true)        // t"hi    "
    TextAlignment.Right.pad(t"hi", 6, true)       // t"    hi"
    TextAlignment.Center.pad(t"hi", 7, true)      // t"  hi   "
    TextAlignment.Justify.pad(t"a b c", 9, false) // t"a   b   c"
    ```

entry .VerticalAlignment   where a cell sits in a row that another cell has made taller: tessellate's `Alignment.Vertical`
    An alias for `tessellate.Alignment.Vertical`, with the cases `Top`, `Middle` and
    `Bottom`. A wrapped cell makes its whole row taller, and the other cells in that row sit
    at the top, middle or bottom of the extra height according to their column's
    `verticalAlign`; `Top` is the default for every type.

entry .TableCell   one cell as rendered: its fitted lines, width, alignment and decoration
  detail
      The renderer's record of a cell after `Layout#tableRow` has fitted it: the lines it
      displays at its column's width, how many there are, how they align, and its decoration.
      A user of `Grid` or `Layout` sees these inside a `TableRow`; nothing else makes them.
  refer escritoire.TableRow
  entry #width   the column's settled width, in cells
  entry #span   how many columns the cell spans; always `1`
  entry #lines   the fitted lines
  entry #minHeight   the number of lines
  entry #textAlign   how each line is padded to the width
  entry #verticalAlign   where the lines sit when the row is taller than `minHeight`
  entry #decorate   the transformation applied to each rendered line, or `Unset`
  entry #apply   the line at an index, which the caller has already checked against `minHeight`

entry .TableRow   one row of fitted cells, with its height in lines
  detail
      Made by `Layout#tableRow`, and rendered by `Grid.rowLines`. The row's height is its
      tallest cell's line count, and a shorter cell is placed within it by its vertical
      alignment.
  refer escritoire.TableCell
  refer escritoire.Grid
  entry #cells   the fitted cells, one per surviving column
  entry #title   whether this is a title row
  entry #height   the number of visual lines the row occupies
  entry #apply   the cell at a column index

entry .TableSection   a run of rows which share column widths
  detail
      A `Grid` is a list of sections, drawn with a rule between each pair. `Tabulation#grid`
      makes two — the titles and the data — at the same widths; every section's rows are
      rendered against the section's own `widths`.
  refer escritoire.Grid
  entry #widths   the column widths the section's rows were fitted to
  entry #rows   the rows, lazily, as a `Chain`
